1
00:00:05,444 --> 00:00:07,680
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:12,400
This time, let's look at the anatomy of the legs and arms.

3
00:00:12,401 --> 00:00:19,040
This time too, we'll briefly look at the anatomy, then at the drawing method.

4
00:00:23,675 --> 00:00:27,480
The thigh can be divided into roughly three masses.

5
00:00:27,481 --> 00:00:28,481
It's made up of several muscles.

6
00:00:29,750 --> 00:00:32,280
Since it looks like this, I won't go into each one.

7
00:00:32,281 --> 00:00:34,040
Please see it as big masses.

8
00:00:34,041 --> 00:00:37,480
Especially, remember these two masses on the inside well.

9
00:00:37,481 --> 00:00:40,760
It's the structure that creates a gap in the thigh.

10
00:00:40,761 --> 00:00:47,560
And the outer mass covers the side of the leg broadly and wraps around to the hips at the back.

11
00:00:47,561 --> 00:00:49,720
It has a fairly complex shape.

12
00:00:50,335 --> 00:00:56,360
From the front, under the influence of the various muscles and bones, it creates a line like this.

13
00:00:57,660 --> 00:01:01,431
Especially, pay attention to this line that sticks out because of the hip joint.

14
00:01:01,820 --> 00:01:07,390
Together with the iliac crest of the pelvis, it makes the line we all know well.

15
00:01:07,391 --> 00:01:10,711
It's one of the areas I especially pay attention to when depicting women.

16
00:01:11,879 --> 00:01:17,880
The more muscle mass the hips have and the less fat, the more the muscle takes on a kidney-bean shape.

17
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:29,701
Below, there's the biceps femoris, which splits lower down and creates a shape where the back of the knee is hollowed out.

18
00:01:30,710 --> 00:01:33,000
At the front, there's the knee.

19
00:01:33,001 --> 00:01:40,120
This knee is made up of various elements like the kneecap, cartilage, and fat joining together into a complex structure.

20
00:01:40,121 --> 00:01:42,760
But do we need to know it in detail?

21
00:01:42,761 --> 00:01:46,001
You just draw the knee sticking out a reasonable amount.

22
00:01:47,314 --> 00:01:51,060
I used to draw people's face shading on the knee.

23
00:01:51,100 --> 00:01:52,100
It was fun.

24
00:01:53,204 --> 00:01:57,470
The calf muscle can be divided into roughly three masses.

25
00:01:57,470 --> 00:02:00,190
The green part in the middle has no muscle.

26
00:02:00,754 --> 00:02:01,950
It's the shin bone.

27
00:02:02,189 --> 00:02:05,310
It connects down to the ankle bone on the inside.

28
00:02:05,384 --> 00:02:07,470
If you touch it yourself, you'll only feel the bone.

29
00:02:08,725 --> 00:02:12,510
If you look at the cross-section, it's like this.

30
00:02:12,511 --> 00:02:14,830
You have to draw it carefully.

31
00:02:14,831 --> 00:02:18,990
Especially when coloring, you shouldn't just draw the calf round.

32
00:02:18,991 --> 00:02:22,090
You have to bring out this protruding part well.

33
00:02:22,190 --> 00:02:25,831
You have to understand the shape of this cross-section and draw it.

34
00:02:27,709 --> 00:02:34,480
Depending on the shapes of the bones and muscles, this red line becomes the most protruding part.

35
00:02:36,465 --> 00:02:39,930
If you look at the calf muscle from behind, it looks like this.

36
00:02:39,931 --> 00:02:40,970
It's called the gastroc.

37
00:02:42,280 --> 00:02:46,040
The inside is longer top to bottom.

38
00:02:46,041 --> 00:02:49,640
The orange part is the famous Achilles tendon.

39
00:02:49,641 --> 00:02:51,560
It's the largest tendon in our body.

40
00:02:51,720 --> 00:02:54,680
It's good to express it strongly when drawing the leg.

41
00:02:55,055 --> 00:03:02,480
If you compare the gastrocnemius with the ankle bone located below, you get the opposite of this.

42
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:05,520
It's a part you have to think about as important.

43
00:03:07,030 --> 00:03:11,640
The foot is made up of difficult curves, so it's a hard part to draw.

44
00:03:11,641 --> 00:03:19,000
In concept art you don't often draw bare feet, but since shoes are modeled on the shape of the foot, you need to be able to draw it.

45
00:03:19,480 --> 00:03:26,680
Especially if you draw the foot wrong, the character looks like it's floating off the ground or the foot is planted in the ground, making it look unstable.

46
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:32,120
When I was a beginner, I struggled a lot because it was hard to make the feet touch the ground.

47
00:03:32,121 --> 00:03:34,040
You have to draw it in line with the ground perspective.

48
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:46,120
For the foot, first draw the ground surface, then build up the shape of the top of the foot broadly.

49
00:03:49,390 --> 00:03:58,010
Also check the position of the ankle bone, then add details.

50
00:04:21,260 --> 00:04:25,420
Practice like this so you can draw a variety of foot shapes.

51
00:04:29,480 --> 00:04:34,241
Also, you need to be able to draw in line with the ground perspective.

52
00:04:35,710 --> 00:04:39,881
There isn't a single line of the leg that's perfectly vertical.

53
00:04:42,390 --> 00:04:45,960
Each one has to have its own slope.

54
00:04:45,961 --> 00:04:50,641
If you look closely, every line has a slope.

55
00:04:52,520 --> 00:04:55,761
For the leg, you have to think of the flow as important.

56
00:04:57,055 --> 00:04:58,055
The inside.

57
00:05:00,655 --> 00:05:04,200
And think of the outside line as important.

58
00:05:06,550 --> 00:05:10,440
First check the slope of the bone, then add flesh.

59
00:05:10,441 --> 00:05:17,400
Looking at it overall, while there's a big flow, the fibula sticks out to the outside.

60
00:05:17,401 --> 00:05:19,960
In a drawing, the role of this bone is important.

61
00:05:20,685 --> 00:05:25,441
Because of this bone, the human leg forms a line like this.

62
00:05:28,595 --> 00:05:31,340
It's the shape where the calf bulges outward.

63
00:05:35,760 --> 00:05:40,160
Let's add flesh on top of this.

64
00:05:40,210 --> 00:05:44,121
You have to draw it so it bulges because of the femur.

65
00:05:45,820 --> 00:05:49,991
The outside of the thigh forms a gentle curve.

66
00:05:50,600 --> 00:05:55,640
And the inside forms a relatively stronger bend.

67
00:05:55,641 --> 00:05:57,880
This is the part I told you is important.

68
00:06:04,510 --> 00:06:12,160
Since the calf bulges outward, it has to start bulging out from the knee area.

69
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:14,720
Please check the slope ratio carefully.

70
00:06:25,685 --> 00:06:30,071
The calf is also curved on the inside and gentle on the outside.

71
00:06:32,065 --> 00:06:37,020
The most protruding part is higher on the outside and lower on the inside.

72
00:06:37,095 --> 00:06:41,221
The ankle bone, on the other hand, is higher on the inside and lower on the outside.

73
00:06:44,035 --> 00:06:45,390
The foot is the same too.

74
00:06:46,245 --> 00:06:49,950
The inside is curved and the outside is gentle.

75
00:06:54,780 --> 00:06:59,350
You can tell if you draw the shape of the sole of the foot.

76
00:06:59,895 --> 00:07:03,760
The inside is curved and the outside is gentle.

77
00:07:08,285 --> 00:07:09,184
Like this.

78
00:07:09,185 --> 00:07:13,370
These lines appear continuously throughout the leg.

79
00:07:13,371 --> 00:07:15,610
Learn to grasp them well so you can draw them.

80
00:07:21,265 --> 00:07:24,090
Now let's draw the leg in a simplified way.

81
00:07:26,115 --> 00:07:29,830
As I always say, first establish the big flow.

82
00:07:30,550 --> 00:07:34,161
Focus on the flow so you can draw it lightly.

83
00:07:39,055 --> 00:07:43,480
In an empty view, you'd draw the heel coming lower to match the perspective, right?

84
00:07:56,350 --> 00:07:57,790
Then let's add flesh.

85
00:08:00,355 --> 00:08:02,750
Draw while checking the ups and downs too.

86
00:08:04,590 --> 00:08:08,631
You have to draw while grasping the flow of the big muscles well.

87
00:08:28,355 --> 00:08:31,310
The hip isn't as round as you'd think.

88
00:08:31,311 --> 00:08:34,270
Draw it as a kidney-bean shape.

89
00:08:34,271 --> 00:08:38,270
When standing straight, the calf should bulge out more than the thigh.

90
00:08:53,885 --> 00:08:58,600
When drawing a woman too, you have to draw while understanding the flow of the big muscles well.

91
00:08:58,601 --> 00:09:03,160
Personally, I don't find appeal in a leg that's stretched out in a perfect straight line.

92
00:09:03,161 --> 00:09:06,481
It's good to bring out a moderate amount of curve.

93
00:09:06,965 --> 00:09:11,031
Like the door, let's draw legs in a variety of compositions.

94
00:09:35,105 --> 00:09:42,581
Especially when drawing women, pay attention to the line that goes from the waist through the pelvis and into the thigh.

95
00:09:42,945 --> 00:09:46,101
You have to work hard to draw beautiful lines.

96
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:08,730
It's also good to understand how the pelvis cuts into the thigh muscle when you're leaning on one leg.

97
00:10:08,731 --> 00:10:12,131
This red line shows it cutting in.

98
00:10:14,990 --> 00:10:22,711
This part has bone, but this blue part here.

99
00:10:23,580 --> 00:10:24,910
It's made of muscle.

100
00:10:27,260 --> 00:10:29,470
The bone cuts into the muscle.

101
00:10:31,525 --> 00:10:34,080
Like this.

102
00:10:34,081 --> 00:10:35,120
Please express it well.

103
00:10:37,235 --> 00:10:44,860
And next, the space between the thighs, which people call the thigh gap. It's good to express the gap between the legs a bit wide.

104
00:10:48,840 --> 00:10:54,440
And when viewed from the front or slightly from below, you can see the hips from the front too.

105
00:10:54,441 --> 00:10:55,441
Like this.

106
00:10:58,060 --> 00:10:59,580
You can't draw it like this.

107
00:10:59,581 --> 00:11:00,780
You have to express it well.

108
00:11:01,825 --> 00:11:02,825
Like this.

109
00:11:07,955 --> 00:11:10,940
The arm, like the leg, is a collection of several muscles.

110
00:11:11,610 --> 00:11:14,700
Let's look at the main big masses.

111
00:11:14,701 --> 00:11:17,740
At the shoulder, there's the deltoid.

112
00:11:17,741 --> 00:11:21,980
It's made of three masses, but we'll look at it as one.

113
00:11:21,981 --> 00:11:28,220
The deltoid connects from the collarbone to the shoulder blade at the back.

114
00:11:28,221 --> 00:11:30,700
Draw it as if it's covering.

115
00:11:32,139 --> 00:11:38,741
There's a gap like this between the pectoralis major and the deltoid.

116
00:11:39,960 --> 00:11:43,731
It's good to express this gap in your drawing too.

117
00:11:45,905 --> 00:11:51,290
In the upper arm, the biceps and triceps would be important.

118
00:11:51,290 --> 00:11:55,770
This is the biceps and this is the triceps.

119
00:11:55,771 --> 00:12:01,050
In the middle, there's the brachialis, which separates the triceps and biceps.

120
00:12:03,240 --> 00:12:14,120
If you look at the inside of the arm, you can see the coracobrachialis and the brachialis splitting the biceps and triceps.

121
00:12:14,121 --> 00:12:15,960
It's good to keep in mind when drawing.

122
00:12:17,630 --> 00:12:31,280
In the forearm, the brachioradialis, which starts at the outside of the upper arm bone and connects to the styloid process of the radius, and the extensor carpi radialis longus are important.

123
00:12:31,281 --> 00:12:33,280
These names are hard.

124
00:12:33,281 --> 00:12:38,400
It's a muscle that's fairly visible from the outside when a man flexes his arm.

125
00:12:38,401 --> 00:12:46,611
Since I draw the arm based on this brachioradialis, it's good to pay attention to it.

126
00:12:46,970 --> 00:12:49,930
And the extensor digitorum is important.

127
00:12:53,020 --> 00:12:57,550
If you extend your arm and move your fingers, you can see it twitch on the arm.

128
00:12:59,690 --> 00:13:04,010
These three muscles are especially visible in the forearm.

129
00:13:04,011 --> 00:13:08,611
The hollow part of the elbow is also related to these muscles.

130
00:13:09,135 --> 00:13:13,450
This muscle group shows up in most men who don't have fat.

131
00:13:16,940 --> 00:13:23,980
The wrist moves organically according to the two bones, the radius and the ulna.

132
00:13:23,981 --> 00:13:32,930
When the palm faces forward, they become parallel, and when you flip the palm, the radius crosses over the ulna.

133
00:13:35,420 --> 00:13:42,391
The ulna stays put, so it's the ulna, and the radius spins around like a yo-yo, so it's the radius. That makes it easy to remember.

134
00:13:43,230 --> 00:13:44,510
That's not actually what it means.

135
00:13:45,765 --> 00:13:52,000
Let's look at the brachioradialis, which I consider important, along with the bone.

136
00:13:52,001 --> 00:13:57,840
It starts on the outside of the upper arm and connects to the end of the radius.

137
00:13:57,841 --> 00:14:01,321
It's easy to remember that it attaches to the thumb side.

138
00:14:02,615 --> 00:14:09,120
As the radius rotates, this brachioradialis attached to the radius rotates along with it.

139
00:14:09,121 --> 00:14:10,720
That's something to be careful about when drawing the arm.

140
00:14:13,775 --> 00:14:19,960
For the hand, first draw the palm, then the flesh of the thumb and pinky sides.

141
00:14:21,555 --> 00:14:26,060
Then draw the fingers with lines.

142
00:14:26,061 --> 00:14:28,540
Also mark the knuckles.

143
00:14:28,541 --> 00:14:30,300
After that, add the flesh.

144
00:14:31,630 --> 00:14:35,310
This is what to keep in mind when drawing the hand.

145
00:14:35,311 --> 00:14:39,431
When the hand closes, the fingertips meet at a single point.

146
00:14:40,970 --> 00:14:42,250
You can't draw it like this.

147
00:14:43,819 --> 00:14:47,171
And you have to understand the position of the finger joints well.

148
00:14:47,640 --> 00:14:54,010
The fingers don't bend at the point where the fingers split, but at the palm part below that.

149
00:14:56,039 --> 00:15:02,120
Like this.

150
00:15:02,121 --> 00:15:06,680
Based on what we've learned, let's draw the arm simply.

151
00:15:06,681 --> 00:15:10,680
If you're a beginner, first draw the skeleton of the arm.

152
00:15:10,681 --> 00:15:12,280
Also draw the skeleton of the hand.

153
00:15:22,769 --> 00:15:23,770
Draw it from various angles.

154
00:15:23,771 --> 00:15:27,930
Draw the brachioradialis, which I said is important.

155
00:15:27,931 --> 00:15:32,250
Start from the outside of the upper arm bone and connect it to the thumb-side bone.

156
00:15:35,825 --> 00:15:38,250
Draw the silhouette of the arm.

157
00:15:38,251 --> 00:15:43,050
Draw the silhouette of the deltoid, biceps, triceps, and elbow-to-forearm.

158
00:15:58,195 --> 00:16:02,981
From behind, the silhouette of the brachialis is important.

159
00:16:09,930 --> 00:16:15,850
Let's also add flesh to the fingers.

160
00:16:15,851 --> 00:16:18,170
Erase the skeleton you drew.

161
00:16:20,855 --> 00:16:24,840
Look at the cross-section lines to understand the shape.

162
00:16:41,980 --> 00:16:45,591
Once you're used to it, draw quickly.

163
00:16:46,540 --> 00:16:49,630
You can also draw the muscles one by one like this.

164
00:16:50,995 --> 00:16:54,980
As you get used to it, draw more and more with just the silhouette.

165
00:17:43,789 --> 00:17:50,110
Draw the arm simply from this angle.

166
00:18:57,915 --> 00:19:05,350
If you study with anatomy references, first examine the reference closely with your eyes, then draw without looking at it.

167
00:19:06,759 --> 00:19:16,721
When you've finished drawing, or when you're stuck and just can't draw it, look at the reference again and analyze where I drew wrong and how to draw it better.

168
00:19:17,289 --> 00:19:21,850
It will be a much better study than copying mindlessly.

169
00:19:23,629 --> 00:19:30,071
Drawing a human figure and then labeling each muscle while looking at anatomy references is also good.

170
00:19:30,959 --> 00:19:33,200
Memorizing the names isn't bad either.

171
00:19:34,749 --> 00:19:40,320
Because the shape of the muscle and the position of the fibers are contained in the name.

172
00:19:40,321 --> 00:19:44,320
So far, we've looked at human anatomy.

173
00:19:44,321 --> 00:19:53,360
Next time, with the human anatomy and simplified drawing methods we've learned so far, we'll draw a trendy figure across the whole body.

174
00:19:53,361 --> 00:19:54,361
Great work.
